WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:06.900
Graphics in general are well suited to handling&nbsp;
opaque objects. We can simulate the lighting on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:13.260
the surface with a simple equation, and voila,&nbsp;
you have a lit object. But transparent volumes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:18.720
present problems, because of their very nature.&nbsp;
There’s no single surface point to approximate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:24.360
in fact if you zoomed in on this puffy little&nbsp;
cloud, what you’d find is lots and lots of tiny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:29.640
droplets of ice and water. Light is bouncing&nbsp;
around in there, and when taken as a whole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.860
the way they reflect and transmit light inside&nbsp;
the cloud is vastly different than a solid object.

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:39.540
It begins with an equation called&nbsp;
Beer’s Law, or the Beer-Lambert Law,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:44.160
and it relates to these kinds of semi&nbsp;
transparent materials. It basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.780
tells you how much light gets absorbed, given the&nbsp;
distance into a medium the light is travelling.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:51.000
So if our scene here is mountainous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:57.360
we can say fog up the scene by applying this&nbsp;
exact equation, exp(-ABSORBTION * distance)

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:01.800
And what happens is, as I crank&nbsp;
that absorption slider up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:06.000
this whole thing fogs up nicely, the&nbsp;
fog that started out in the distance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.700
starts to creep closer in a somewhat natural&nbsp;
looking way. And it’s no coincidence that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:15.600
this basic calculation is one of the&nbsp;
classic fog calculations in graphics.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.100
And in this case, there’s not much you need&nbsp;
to know beyond how far away the point is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.080
in order to do the fog calculation.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.680
If we were to say, bring an&nbsp;
object onto the screen instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.120
and we want it to be a somewhat cloudlike&nbsp;
material, this is a bit different.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:34.260
If you look at a side view of the&nbsp;
cube, here’s your eye over here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:38.880
and you’re looking in this direction, you&nbsp;
need to figure out how far through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.540
object the ray has travelled,&nbsp;
in order to apply Beer's law.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:48.420
Which means figuring out where it enters, and&nbsp;
then figuring out where it leaves. Using those,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:52.920
you can calculate the distance in-between and&nbsp;
apply Beer's law using the equation above.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:58.500
Given this distance then, you can apply Beer’s&nbsp;
law using the equation above, and voila,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:02.100
kinda giving you that impression&nbsp;
of a semi transparent interior.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.800
So yet another problem is that we’ve made an&nbsp;
assumption here, that the density along this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:14.580
distance here was completely constant, that it&nbsp;
was the same the entire time. What if it isn’t?

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:19.380
That means you can’t just blindly take this&nbsp;
distance, you have to do a bit more work. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:25.320
next step then, is to take this vector, and divide&nbsp;
it into a bunch of segments, then at each of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:31.080
in sequence, you can sample your density, multiply&nbsp;
that by the segment length, and add it all up.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:34.980
Then you can apply beer’s law, and now&nbsp;
you’ve got kinda swirls and other stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:40.080
happening inside this sort of cloudy interior,&nbsp;
so we’re making some very cool progress here.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:45.060
It’s very flat though, there’s no depth to&nbsp;
it. If we introduce a light to the scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:49.980
well, nothing happens. We’re ray marching&nbsp;
through our little density field here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.100
but we don’t really have a&nbsp;
way to react to lighting.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:58.200
We already know that the further into the medium&nbsp;
the light travels, the more it’s being absorbed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:03.000
this is what we use Beer’s Law for.&nbsp;
But what we haven’t figured out is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.340
how much light actually gets to any given point.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:10.620
So if you have a light source up here, at each of&nbsp;
these points along this path that you’re sampling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:14.820
you need to figure out how much light energy&nbsp;
there is by the time you get to that point.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:22.140
And similar to before, since this medium isn’t&nbsp;
uniformly dense, what you need to do is, starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:27.360
from each point along here, you need to do a&nbsp;
second set of samples towards the light source.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:31.080
Once that code is in place, we can&nbsp;
see the lighting starting to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:34.140
effect on our cloudy object,&nbsp;
it's looking better already.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:39.420
Jiggle the light around a bit and the&nbsp;
cloudy interior seemingly gets lit properly.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:44.820
Although if you swing the light source behind&nbsp;
our cloudy object, you don’t get that distinctive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:49.080
silver lining that real clouds are famous for.&nbsp;
You’re getting a bit of light bleeding through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:54.060
but it’s not the same. This is more&nbsp;
smoke than clouds. Why is that?

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:59.100
So if we look at a ray of light passing&nbsp;
through the cloud, in reality it’s going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:04.320
to get scattered by the water droplets inside.&nbsp;
until now, we’ve actually been assuming that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:08.220
lighting coming into the cloud is getting&nbsp;
distributed pretty evenly in all directions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:14.880
This is called isotropic scattering. But&nbsp;
in real clouds, as light enters the cloud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:19.980
it doesn’t scatter so evenly, it actually has&nbsp;
a higher probability of scattering forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:22.860
and this is called anisotropic scattering.

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:26.940
So you use something called a&nbsp;
phase function to simulate that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:32.580
and a common one is the Henyey-Greenstein&nbsp;
model. If we plug this directly into our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:36.780
code and multiply the light energy at any&nbsp;
given point by this new phase function

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:40.080
What we end up seeing is better directional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:43.080
scattering as we move the light&nbsp;
around, which is really cool.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:48.060
One of the interesting insights that the&nbsp;
original engineers at Guerrilla Games had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:52.260
was that clouds also have sort of&nbsp;
darker edges and lighter creases.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:55.440
As light enters a cloud, it begins to scatter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.980 --> 00:05:02.220
Points closer to the surface scatter deeper into&nbsp;
the cloud. Points further inside the cloud have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:07.740
actually received more scattered light than&nbsp;
points closer to the surface. The end result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:13.080
being that points in cracks and crevices may have&nbsp;
gathered more light than points near the surface.

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:17.340
The Guerrilla Games engineers called&nbsp;
this the powdered sugar effect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:19.620
because it looks like powdered sugar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:25.020
and their simple fix was to modify beer’s law&nbsp;
a bit and drop off the energy at the start.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:28.620
So you’ve got exp(-d), which is Beer's law&nbsp;

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:33.660
And you’ve got this new&nbsp;
Powder equation, 1 - e^-d*2

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:37.920
And the combined equation&nbsp;
might look something like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:40.620
which they called the Beer’s Powder approximation.

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:46.680
And in the following years, other papers came&nbsp;
out improving the look even further. The folks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:51.660
working on the Frostbite engine for EA released&nbsp;
a bunch of improvements to the original clouds.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:57.120
By mixing between a forward and backward&nbsp;
Henyey-Greenstein function, they were able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:01.440
to simulate a back scattering component&nbsp;
to clouds, improving the look quite a bit.

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:06.960
There was also a neat paper from some&nbsp;
engineers at Sony Pictures working on Oz:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:11.940
The Great and Powerful, who noted that if&nbsp;
they took multiple samples of the scattering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:16.860
lowering the extinction for each, and summed&nbsp;
them, it would allow more light through.

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:21.660
It basically looks like this, which you&nbsp;
drop in as a replacement for the Beer’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:23.760
law calculation when summing up the lighting.

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:29.220
So we’ve got our little object, and we’ve walked&nbsp;
through most of the calculations necessary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:33.600
all that’s left is actually shaping this&nbsp;
thing to be more cloud-like in appearance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:39.720
And that’s where noise comes in. Remember&nbsp;
noise? One of my favourite youtube channels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:43.140
the Bob’s Burgers guy, just&nbsp;
covered noise in detail.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:47.940
The original paper specified they used&nbsp;
something called Perlin-Worley noise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:53.640
which is a combo of the 2 noises, although they&nbsp;
don’t specify exactly how they’re combined.

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:59.040
So for Worley, you start with this basic&nbsp;
grid, and at every cell in the grid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:03.780
you place a point at the centre.&nbsp;
Then, using a noise or hash function,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:08.040
you offset it by some point, moving&nbsp;
it away, how far is up to you.

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:13.440
Then, to calculate the noise for any given&nbsp;
pixel, you figure out which cell you’re in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:18.240
and which cells are adjacent. You loop through&nbsp;
each of them, figuring out the distance between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:22.680
your sample point and the neighbouring cell’s&nbsp;
offset point, and take the min of all of those.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:28.860
You end up with something that looks like this,&nbsp;
kinda organic looking. By inverting the colour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:36.060
you get puffy, billowy shapes. The downside is&nbsp;
that this doesn’t tile, at least not at first, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:42.900
that’s actually a really simple fix, you modulo&nbsp;
the cell offsets so that they repeat. I was able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:49.140
to add this single line to the Worley calculation,&nbsp;
and voila, repetition in all directions.

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:55.980
Perlin has a similar tiling problem, you can&nbsp;
see that an FBM texture doesn’t tile well here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.520 --> 00:08:02.100
And unsurprisingly, has a similar solution,&nbsp;
a mod on positions in the hash function,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:08.040
and scale that while generating each octave of&nbsp;
the FBM, and you’ve got some nice tiling going on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:10.860
Easy peasy lemon squeezy.

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:15.060
This is where things got pretty&nbsp;
hand-wavy from the original paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:21.900
luckily the Frostbite team goes into more&nbsp;
detail. They used multiple octaves of Worley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:27.420
noise to create what’s basically a version&nbsp;
of worley FBM, or fractional brownian motion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:32.220
Then, they used that to remap perlin noise&nbsp;
to get this slightly cauliflower appearance.

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:37.560
Stuffing this into a 3d texture, and&nbsp;
then using that to carve away pieces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:40.380
and you start to see the&nbsp;
cloud-like features showing up.

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:47.460
So now the really neat thing is that you can make&nbsp;
pretty much any shape you want. If I use SDF’s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:53.940
I can represent cubes, donuts, or even more&nbsp;
complex objects. The next issue is that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:57.840
right now, I’ve had to constrain the&nbsp;
bounds of my cloudy area pretty small,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:02.760
in fact the bounds of the area that I’m sampling&nbsp;
just barely cover what’s being shown on screen.

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:07.860
If I move the cloud, you quickly see that&nbsp;
it runs into the edges of the bounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:12.840
but if I expand the bounds out too far,&nbsp;
let’s say 20x the size of the area,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:15.780
it’s quickly apparent that&nbsp;
the approach falls apart.

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:20.760
Massively upping the number of samples&nbsp;
improves the look, but tanks the frame rate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:26.940
Assuming the clouds are implemented properly, at&nbsp;
this point then, it’s mostly an optimization game.

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:31.440
This is where the authors talk about using&nbsp;
various techniques to speed things up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:36.720
They describe using a cheaper sampling&nbsp;
approach with larger steps, and if and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:41.100
when they hit a cloud surface, they backtrack&nbsp;
and then start using high quality sampling.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:47.100
I took this a bit further by using signed&nbsp;
distance fields to describe the clouds' surfaces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:51.540
and used sphere tracing (check out my&nbsp;
video on ray marching for more details),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:56.220
and then I switch to a high quality sampler&nbsp;
when I’m within a certain threshold of a cloud.

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:00.840
I also experimented with running&nbsp;
this at half resolution or lower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:05.460
and just compositing the result. I&nbsp;
mean, it’s a cloud, so the details&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:10.380
aren’t super well defined anyway, or at&nbsp;
least that’s my hope. It looks alright.

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:14.160
There’s wayyyyy more you can do here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:20.220
I know the Frostbite paper describes amortising&nbsp;
across multiple frames using temporal scattering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:24.900
integration and reprojection to speed things up,&nbsp;
but I think I’m going to throw in the towel here.

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:29.520
This was a fun little project, and I’m&nbsp;
certain that I got a bunch of things wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:35.220
but it looks cool, so I can’t complain.&nbsp;
It’s also an amazing illustration of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:39.540
sheer amount of hard work that goes into&nbsp;
just background details in modern games.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:42.660
Code will be up on github at some point.

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:43.500
Cheers

